WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:12.130
Alfred Nobel

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.100
We remember him for the annual prizes that
he gifted to the world, but Alfred Nobel also

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.340
left us with another legacy - explosives.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.230
He was the inventor of dynamite and its even
more deadly incarnation, gelignite.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:29.230
They made him rich beyond his wildest dreams,
but also left him with a tortured conscience.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:36.880
In this week’s Biographics, we examine the
life of Alfred Nobel.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:45.410
Early Years

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.940
Alfred Bernhard Nobel was born on October
21st, 1833 in Stockholm, Sweden.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.530
He was a sickly child who almost died during
the first week of birth.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.380
As it was, he was one of just four out of
eight children to survive.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.690
His illness kept him indoors while his three
brothers played outside.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.210
Alfred’s father, Immanuel, was an inventor
and engineer, but he struggled to realize

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.040
his ambitions and, as a result, the family
struggled to get by.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.850
Just three months prior to Alfred’s birth,
his father had been forced to declare bankruptcy

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.330
and shut down his business.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.549
Immanuel was now being chased by creditors.

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:21.750
Things became almost unbearable when a fire
ravaged the family home.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:26.460
To help make ends meet, the Nobel children
were sent out onto the streets to sell matches.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.330
Alfred was an extremely pallid, sickly child.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:34.960
He suffered from epileptic convulsions along
with a host of gastric diseases and debilitating

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:35.960
migraine headaches.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.250
For the first six years of his life, Alfred
was schooled by his mother.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.770
He proved himself to be a gifted student from
the start.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.030
By the age of three he was able to read.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.210
From that time onward, he was rarely seen
without a book in hand.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.630
He also had a great memory.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.510
When Alfred was four year old, his father
moved to Russia in order to pursue a business

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.510
venture.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.610
Immanuel had managed to get a meeting with
the Chairman of the Czar’s Committee for

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.920
the Promotion of Industry and Trade.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.140
He steered the conversation towards landmines,
his area of expertise, and was able to secure

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.689
an invitation to Turku to meet some influential
people.

00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:14.049
Immanuel worked in Turku for two years, where
he invented the product we know today as plywood.

00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:16.069
He then moved to St. Petersburg.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.780
There he was introduced to Czar Nicholas 1st.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.590
Seizing the opportunity, Immanuel told the
Czar about his plans to develop a submerged

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.010
explosive mine.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:29.519
Nicholas immediately saw the potential to
use the invention to prevent enemy ships from

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:32.230
entering into Russian waters.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:37.700
A Change of Fortune

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.590
Things were now looking up for the Nobels.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:43.770
With the money he received from the Russian
government as compensation for his development

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:49.430
of the submerged explosive mine, Immanuel
set up a mechanical workshop and armaments

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.430
factory.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.690
As well as making the new mine, the factory
also produced industrial tools and cannon

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.690
shells.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:00.049
In 1842, when, Alfred was nine years old,
Immanuel sent for his family to join him from

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:01.049
Stockholm.

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:05.180
The new life of the Nobel’s was vastly different
to what they had known in Sweden.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.849
Their St. Petersburg house was luxurious in
comparison to their old home.

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:14.079
Immanuel was now able to hire a tutor for
his sons.Unlike his brothers, Alfred was studious

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:15.489
and intellectual.

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:18.419
He was also intensely curious about the world
around him.

00:03:18.419 --> 00:03:23.230
He loved the poetry of Percy Shelley, especially
those concerned with the subjects of nature

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.749
and justice.

00:03:24.749 --> 00:03:29.390
From these readings, he developed a deep desire
to stand up to injustice.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.329
Alfred’s other loves were chemistry and
languages.

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:35.680
By the time he was seventeen, he could speak
five languages.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.809
His father also recognized in the boy a natural
affinity for solving problems, marking Alfred

00:03:40.809 --> 00:03:44.870
as the most promising of his sons to follow
in his inventive footsteps.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.109
The boy loved nothing better than to spend
his time in his father’s factory, silently

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:51.449
taking in everything that his dad was doing.

00:03:51.449 --> 00:03:55.549
Before long, his father allowed him to handle
the chemicals himself.

00:03:55.549 --> 00:03:59.530
Alfred’s tutors encouraged his passion for
chemistry and literature.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:03.370
At the same time they coaxed him to come out
of his shell socially.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.299
Largely as a result of his ill health he was
a painfully shy and introverted boy.

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:11.089
Yet, with the help of his tutors, he began
to stretch himself academically in his mid

00:04:11.089 --> 00:04:12.089
teens.

00:04:12.089 --> 00:04:16.480
He began participating in debating clubs and
started to share his passions with family

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.480
members.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.760
Then, around the age of 15, he had a growth
spurt which put him at the height of most

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:22.760
of his peers.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.510
This went a long way to boost his confidence.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.530
In 1850, Immanuel paid for Alfred to study
in Paris.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.980
He had already been studying with chemist
Nikolai Zinin, and it was chemistry that he

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.770
pursued in France.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.190
The Nitro Fascination

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:46.150
In Paris, Alfred spent time with Ascanio Sobrero,
who had recently invented nitroglycerin.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:51.320
Sobrero was totally opposed to any commercial
use of his invention due to its highly unpredictable

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:52.790
explosive potential.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.980
In fact, in later life he made the following
statement about his invention . . .

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.260
When I think of all the victims killed during
nitroglycerine explosions, and the terrible

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:05.030
havoc that it has wreaked, which in all probability
will continue to occur in the future, I am

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.450
almost ashamed to admit to being its discoverer.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:12.510
Sobrero’s aversion to his discovery did
not put off young Nobel.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.860
In fact, the reverse was true - the substance
fascinated Alfred.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:20.310
Nobel also met a chemist by the name of Jules
Pelouze, who was fascinated with nitroglycerin

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.470
and had a determination to bring it under
control.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.870
After a year in France, Alfred went to the
United States to pursue his studies.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.950
There he worked with Swedish inventor John
Ericcson.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:34.810
Ericcson would go on to gain fame as the inventor
of ironclad warships used in the civil war

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.400
as well as the inventor of the screw propellor.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.610
Erricson filled Alfred’s mind with dreams
of what he could do if he was able to tame

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.310
nitroglycerin.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.780
After a year in America, Alfred returned to
St. Petersburg.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.720
His family recognized a changed young man.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:49.960
His brother Ludwig recalled . . .

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.360
Alfred had grown so that I hardly knew him.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.740
He is almost as tall as I, and his voice has
become so deep and strong that I didn’t

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:58.740
recognize it.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.290
But Alfred did more than change physically.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:05.810
The young man who had left with lots of ideas
but little direction now had a goal - to turn

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:10.860
nitroglycerin into a commercially usable blasting
agent for use in tunnels and to create fear

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.970
inspiring weapons of war that were so terrible
that nations would be deterred from ever going

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:16.970
to war.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:20.000
First, though, Alfred had to help out the
family.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.810
He worked alongside his brothers in one of
his father’s factories.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:26.240
But the physical work proved too much for
his weakened body.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:27.930
He was often too sick to work.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:28.930
On one occasion.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:34.060
in 1854, he became so unwell that his father
packed him off to the spa town on Franzenbad

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.320
to recuperate.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.580
By now, Immanuel’s business was flourishing.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:42.160
His submerged explosive mine was in high demand,
along with other munitions, to supply the

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.840
Russian forces fighting in the Crimean War
of 1853 through 56.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.330
By 1853, Immanuel was employing more than
a thousand people in his factories and the

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:56.710
Russian government was so pleased with him
that they awarded him the Imperial Gold Medal

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.310
in recognition of his ‘diligence and creative
skill in Russian industry’.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:06.560
In between overseeing his manufacturing empire,
Immanuel found time to collaborate with Alfred

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.280
on the nitroglycerine problem.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:13.680
But even, Immanuel, with his vast experience,
could not find a safe way to detonate the

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:14.680
substance.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:20.340
The end of the Crimean War in 1856 saw a sharp
downturn in business for the Nobel company.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.670
Immanuel was forced to change his focus from
armaments production to churning out household

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.410
and industrial implements.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.310
But still the business suffered.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:32.460
By 1859, Immanuel found himself facing bankruptcy
for the second time.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:37.330
He closed the factory, liquidated his assets
and moved with his wife and youngest son,

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.590
Emil, back to Sweden.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:43.280
Alfred and his two older brother, Ludwig and
Robert, stayed on in St. Petersburg.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.150
Twenty-eight year old Ludwig was given the
job of consolidating and running the remnant

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.340
of the Nobel’s Russian operation.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.740
He rebranded the company as the Ludwig Nobel
Mechanical Factory.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.280
Alfred spent about four hours each day helping
out with the family business.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.150
The rest of the day he worked on his passion
to tame nitroglycerine.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.290
Dynamite!

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:10.190
Alfred’s first step was to learn to create
nitroglycerin himself.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:12.470
By 1861, he had accomplished that goal.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:16.419
After spending up to 18 hours a day in a small
factory building, the first breakthrough came

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:23.110
when he discovered how to transport nitroglycerin
safely - by first soaking it in coal dust.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:26.370
Now, suddenly, nitroglycerin was a viable
explosive.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.919
Engineering companies from all over the world
came to the Nobel Company to purchase the

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:32.290
product - and the money started rolling in.

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:36.839
Alfred’s next challenge was to find a safe
way to detonate nitroglycerin.

00:08:36.839 --> 00:08:42.149
In September, 1864 tragedy struck the Swedish
Nobel company factory.

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:44.439
A vat of nitroglycerin had been overheated.

00:08:44.439 --> 00:08:49.140
In the resulting explosion five people were
killed, including Alfred’s younger brother,

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.140
Emil.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.059
Alfred, himself, was working in the building
next door, and he suffered minor injuries

00:08:53.059 --> 00:08:54.420
in the disaster.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:09:00.339
Rather than being put off working with nitroglycerin,
Alfred threw himself into making the explosive

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:01.339
safer.

00:09:01.339 --> 00:09:05.550
To give up now would be ,in his view, to allow
his brother to have died in vain.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.249
In 1865, he built a factory in Hamburg, Germany.

00:09:08.249 --> 00:09:13.370
It so happened that the soil around the Hamburg
factory was special - it contained minute

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.040
particles of fossils.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.350
When nitroglycerin was poured onto the soil,
it turned it into a doughy consistency.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:23.019
This process, in effect, put the nitroglycerin
to sleep.

00:09:23.019 --> 00:09:27.320
In order to wake it up for detonation purposes,
Nobel invented the blasting cap.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:32.150
Alfred knew that a fuse could not be used
directly to detonate nitroglycerin, but that

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.170
gunpowder could detonate it.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:38.740
A fuse could be used to detonate the gunpowder
which would then ignite the nitroglycerin.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:43.560
By 1867, he had mastered the use of small
copper capsules of mercury fulminate, which

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.899
was ignited by a fuse to detonate nitroglycerin.

00:09:46.899 --> 00:09:52.629
This blasting cap would wake up the nitroglycerin
which was packed in a doughy soil base.

00:09:52.629 --> 00:09:57.449
Nobel called the combination of nitroglycerin,
blasting cap and soil ‘dynamite’.

00:09:57.449 --> 00:10:00.470
Nobel patented dynamite and began to put it
on the commercial market.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:04.810
It was an immediate success, with engineers
from all over the world clamoring to get their

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:05.810
hands on it.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:12.369
Virtually overnight, Alfred became rich beyond
his wildest dreams.

00:10:12.369 --> 00:10:16.990
Fabulous Wealth

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:22.339
We can get an idea of high much money came
in by an anecdote, apparently true, concerning

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:23.870
his housemaid’s wedding.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.600
When Alfred asked her what she would like
for a gift, the very astute girl replied,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.019
‘As much as you make in a day’.

00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:34.910
The following day, Nobel gave her a check
for $100,000.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:39.089
Alfred himself never married, though he did
have at least three great loves throughout

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:40.089
his life.

00:10:40.089 --> 00:10:43.230
While living in Russia in his early twenties
he fell for a Russian girl by the name of

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.240
Alexandria.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.619
Painfully shy, he finally built up the gumption
to tell the girl how he felt about her, but

00:10:49.619 --> 00:10:52.020
his affections were roundly rejected.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.939
The rebuff hurt him badly, and for the next
twenty years, he refused to let his heart

00:10:55.939 --> 00:10:57.139
be broken again.

00:10:57.139 --> 00:11:00.810
Nobel consciously portrayed himself as a hermit
and an eccentric.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.040
He once wrote the following about himself
. . .

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.040
I am a misanthrope and yet utterly benevolent,
have more than one screw loose, yet am a super

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.130
idealist who digests philosophy more efficiently
than food.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:17.850
Even though he had enough money to purchase
anything he wanted, Alfred was rather frugal.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:22.379
This may have been a reflection of the extreme
poverty that he experienced in his childhood.

00:11:22.379 --> 00:11:26.860
He had no interest in purchasing such personal
tokens of wealth as elegant carriages, clothing

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:31.300
or food, but would invest liberally in new
estates and business ventures.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:32.879
Nobel was known to have a temper.

00:11:32.879 --> 00:11:37.209
He was a man who did not suffer fools with
many occasions being recorded when he was

00:11:37.209 --> 00:11:39.499
rude with people, usually those who worked
for him.

00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:43.860
He was a get to the point type of guy, yet
he also had the ability to charm potential

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:47.829
investors when the need warranted it.

00:11:47.829 --> 00:11:53.149
The Women in His Life

00:11:53.149 --> 00:11:57.149
In 1876, Alfred, apparently tired of his life
of solitude, placed an advertisement in the

00:11:57.149 --> 00:11:58.899
newspaper that read as follows . . .

00:11:58.899 --> 00:12:04.670
Wealthy, highly educated gentleman seeks lady
of mature age, versed in languages as secretary

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.610
and supervisor of household.

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:10.519
After interviewing a number of applicants,
Alfred employed a woman by the name of Bertha

00:12:10.519 --> 00:12:11.519
Kinsky.

00:12:11.519 --> 00:12:15.779
Thirty three year old Bertha was extremely
attractive and Nobel was immediately attracted

00:12:15.779 --> 00:12:16.779
to her.

00:12:16.779 --> 00:12:19.629
He was also taken with her obvious intelligence
and wit.

00:12:19.629 --> 00:12:22.509
But Bertha did not return her employer's affections.

00:12:22.509 --> 00:12:24.610
In fact, she had a fiancee.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:29.689
After a few months, probably feeling ill at
ease with the situation, she resigned as Nobel’s

00:12:29.689 --> 00:12:30.689
secretary.

00:12:30.689 --> 00:12:34.389
Still, the two remained friends and they maintained
an active letter correspondence right up until

00:12:34.389 --> 00:12:35.800
the time of Nobel’s death.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:40.559
Bertya, in fact, became the recipient of the
Nobel Peace Prize in 1905.

00:12:40.559 --> 00:12:45.209
Some weeks after Bertha left his employ, Nobel
met a young woman by the name of Sofie Hess.

00:12:45.209 --> 00:12:50.209
Despite the fact that Alfred was 43 and Sofie
was just twenty they entered into a relationship

00:12:50.209 --> 00:12:52.290
that was to last for eighteen years.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.959
Apparently embarrassed by the age gap, Nobel
kept the relationship secret.

00:12:55.959 --> 00:12:59.949
The age difference was not the only thing
that seemed to work against the relationship.

00:12:59.949 --> 00:13:03.019
Sofie was uncouth, uneducated and ill mannered.

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:05.019
Their relationship was a toxic one.

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:10.619
In 1891, Sofie became pregnant to another
man but Alfred held out hope for their relationship

00:13:10.619 --> 00:13:13.950
until she married the child’s father three
years later.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:20.269
Even then, he sent her an annual allowance
of 6,000 florins.

00:13:20.269 --> 00:13:24.040
International Businessman

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:25.959
Nobel never had any children.

00:13:25.959 --> 00:13:29.970
Despite his immense wealth, he filled his
days with work, often going nonstop for twenty

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:30.970
hours.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:36.110
In 1875 he invented gelignite, which proved
to be a safer explosive than dynamite.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:39.660
In the late 1860’s Alfred set up a number
of plants across Europe and the United States.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:45.189
In June, 1866, he started the United States
Blasting Oil Company, the first nitro company

00:13:45.189 --> 00:13:46.189
in the US.

00:13:46.189 --> 00:13:50.279
He also spent increasing amounts of time and
money protecting his patented inventions from

00:13:50.279 --> 00:13:53.739
fraudsters who tried to make money off his
hard work.

00:13:53.739 --> 00:13:57.940
Alfred was convinced that his invention of
dynamite would ultimately be for the good

00:13:57.940 --> 00:13:59.449
of mankind, saying that . . .

00:13:59.449 --> 00:14:03.769
My dynamite will sooner lead to peace than
a thousand world conventions.

00:14:03.769 --> 00:14:08.800
As soon as men will find that in one instant
, whole armes can be utterly destroyed, they

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:11.810
surely will abide in golden peace.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:16.329
As history has borne out, his faith in mankind
was sadly misplaced.

00:14:16.329 --> 00:14:21.610
In 1869, while traveling in France, Nobel
met Paul Barbe, a graduate of the Ecole Polytechnique.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:27.029
Barbe, who came from an affluent family, was
fascinated with Nobel’s dynamite and proposed

00:14:27.029 --> 00:14:28.029
a partnership.

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:31.829
He offered to finance the production of dynamite
in France and told Nobel that he wanted to

00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:33.809
learn how to produce it himself.

00:14:33.809 --> 00:14:37.429
He had Nobel’s interest, mainly because
France was envied in the industry for the

00:14:37.429 --> 00:14:40.389
state monopoly that it held over explosives
production.

00:14:40.389 --> 00:14:45.040
The two men came to an agreement by which
all profits would be split 50:50.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.699
The French enterprise was a success and had
soon expanded to factories in Spain, Switzerland

00:14:49.699 --> 00:14:50.809
and Italy.

00:14:50.809 --> 00:14:54.569
In 1868, Alfred set his sights on establishing
a dynamite company in England.

00:14:54.569 --> 00:14:59.619
He considered this to be the prime location,
as it would open up the whole British Empire

00:14:59.619 --> 00:15:00.619
as his new market.

00:15:00.619 --> 00:15:04.620
He spent several months travelling around
Britain’s mining districts looking for suitable

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:05.990
factory locations.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:08.679
The best chances for investment came from
Scotland.

00:15:08.679 --> 00:15:12.399
After much negotiation, the British Dynamite
Company was founded.

00:15:12.399 --> 00:15:15.970
In 1871, Alfred purchased a four storey mansion
and estate in Paris.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:21.529
As well as a greenhouse for his prize orchids
and stables for his beloved horses, the estate

00:15:21.529 --> 00:15:22.649
had a private laboratory.

00:15:22.649 --> 00:15:26.749
For the next decade, Alfred spent the bulk
of his time working in this lab, along with

00:15:26.749 --> 00:15:28.619
his assistant Georges Fehrenbach.

00:15:28.619 --> 00:15:34.749
It was here that he discovered what he considered
to be dynamite 2.0 - gelignite.

00:15:34.749 --> 00:15:38.740
Gelignite was not only more stable than dynamite
- it caused a greater explosion and was easier

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:39.740
to shape.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:43.269
Overall, gelignite was also much safer and
easier to handle than dynamite.

00:15:43.269 --> 00:15:47.389
The new invention was an even greater success
than dynamite and it caused even more money

00:15:47.389 --> 00:15:49.329
to flow into the Nobel coffers.

00:15:49.329 --> 00:15:54.060
In 1881, Alfred had grown dissatisfied with
the facilities in his Parisa mansion laboratory.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:58.660
He bought another estate, this one located
some 15 miles from Paris in the town of Sevran.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:00.999
Here he built a larger and better equipped
lab.

00:16:00.999 --> 00:16:05.179
He based himself at Sevran, but ran into problems
with the French authorities when he refused

00:16:05.179 --> 00:16:09.860
to comply with their new regulations and policies,
which he considered to be unnecessarily restrictive.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:14.300
Amazingly, the French government charged him
with ‘high crimes and treason’.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.389
He was forced to close his new lab in Sevran
and left for Italy.

00:16:17.389 --> 00:16:21.029
The French government forbade him from ever
working in their country again.

00:16:21.029 --> 00:16:26.220
Prior to his ouster from France, Alfred had
been working on his latest invention, ballistite.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:29.480
He continued this work at his new lab in San
Remo, Italy.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:34.629
Ballistite was a smokeless propellant that
combined nitrocellulose and nitroglycerine.

00:16:34.629 --> 00:16:38.209
The rights to the new explosive were leased
to the Italian government, who began using

00:16:38.209 --> 00:16:44.709
M1890 Vetterli rifles, which used cartridges
that were loaded with ballistite.

00:16:44.709 --> 00:16:49.240
A Shocking Realization

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.120
Alfred’s brother Ludwig died in 1888.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:57.239
The newspapers, however, reported that it
was the far more famous Alfred who had passed

00:16:57.239 --> 00:16:58.239
away.

00:16:58.239 --> 00:17:00.889
As a result, Nobel had the rare experience
of reading his own obituary.

00:17:00.889 --> 00:17:03.920
What he read shocked him to the core.

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:09.370
Virtually every newspaper that he looked at
seemed to glory in his supposed demise.

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:10.860
One French headline announced . . .

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:13.350
The Merchant of Death is Dead.

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:14.350
Another report stated . . .

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:19.110
Dr. Alfred Nobel (the mutilator), who became
rich by finding ways to kill more people faster

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:21.530
than ever before, died yesterday.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:26.271
Nobel was devastated at the realization that
the end result of his life's work was to be

00:17:26.271 --> 00:17:27.950
worldwide condemnation.

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:32.590
He decided that he had to do something to
turn around his reputation in whatever time

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:33.590
he had left.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:38.440
In 1893, Alfred employed a personal assistant
by the name of Ragnar Solman and and appointed

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.740
him as executor of his will.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:44.160
The two men worked diligently on the document
over the next two years.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:50.080
Then, on November 27th, 1895, Alfred signed
the will at the Swedish-Norwegian Club in

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.080
Paris.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:52.100
It was a will like no other..

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:59.179
Alfred left 94 percent of his total wealth
- the equivalent of 265 million 2018 dollars

00:17:59.179 --> 00:18:04.000
- to the awarding of a number of annual prizes
to individuals who, according to the will

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:05.000
. . .

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:09.050
during the preceding year, have conferred
the greatest benefit on mankind.

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:14.460
The will spelled out five categories - physics,
medicine, chemistry, literature and peace.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.970
The peace prize was to to be given to . . .

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:20.890
The person who has done the most, or the best
work, for fraternity between the nations and

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:26.640
abolition or reduction of standing armies
and the formation and spreading of peace congresses.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.140
Today, there is a sixth category, economics,
but this was not added until the end of the

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.520
20th century.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:38.280
Death and a Legacy

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:42.240
In his late 50’s, Nobel’s health issues
increasingly caused him problems.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.800
His heart was especially weak.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:48.170
In an attempt to self medicate, he began administering
doses of nitroglycerin.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:51.480
This quite likely contributed to his deteriorating
state.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:56.500
His body finally gave out on December, 10,
1896., following a stroke.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.340
He was 63 years of age.

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:45.830
The Nobel Prizes began to be awarded

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:56.280
in

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:35.950
five

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:40.290
years later.

